<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE module PUBLIC
    "-//Checkstyle//DTD Checkstyle Configuration 1.3//EN"
    "https://checkstyle.org/dtds/configuration_1_3.dtd">

<!--
=============================================================================
config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Purpose:
  The shared Checkstyle rule set that mechanically enforces this project's
  single user-specified rule, Rule 1 (Explainability), for Java.

  Rule 1 and the frozen Agent Action Plan are authoritative. This file and
  the prose standard in docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md are both
  IMPLEMENTATIONS of Rule 1, and neither may weaken it, narrow its scope or
  grant an exemption Rule 1 does not grant. Where this configuration, that
  standard and Rule 1 disagree, Rule 1 decides, and whichever of the other
  two fell short is corrected UPWARD to meet it, so a gap found here is
  fixed here, and is never treated as licence to relax the standard.
    Alternatives Considered: making this configuration authoritative over
      the prose, on the model tests/README.md uses for its runner scripts.
      Rejected. That precedence is sound where a script is the artifact that
      runs and the prose only describes it, but a user-specified rule is a
      REQUIREMENT and a linter is a partial test of it. Letting the test
      define the requirement means every limitation of Checkstyle silently
      becomes an exemption from Rule 1, which is exactly the shape of
      failure the honest-limits note at the foot of this file exists to
      prevent.
    Assumptions: this file still cannot express every clause of Rule 1, and
      the remainder is carried by review rather than deleted. What each
      module does and does not reach is stated at the module.

  Two consumers read this file. Both are named here, with what they do,
  so that this file can be understood without opening either of them.
    1. services/pom.xml binds maven-checkstyle-plugin to the Maven
       validate phase with this file as its configuration. It pins the
       Checkstyle engine to 13.8.0, overriding the plugin's older bundled
       engine, and it supplies config_loc as the directory that holds this
       file. Consequently the gate runs on every local build and not only
       in CI, and it audits main and test sources alike.
    2. .github/workflows/services-ci.yml runs that same binding as a
       required, build-failing step.

  One copy of this rule set governs all nine Maven modules under
  services/: common-lib, plus the auth, account, card, transaction,
  reference, batch, authorization and reporting services.

  config/checkstyle/suppressions.xml is the companion file. This rule set
  names it, and only it, as the single place a suppression may be recorded.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Scope:
  This file enforces Javadoc presence, Javadoc completeness and comment
  adjacency. It configures nothing else. There are deliberately no naming,
  whitespace, import-order, line-length, metrics or header modules here.
  Every module left out is listed, with the reason it was left out, in the
  EXCLUDED MODULES section at the foot of this file.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
What this rule set cannot check: it does NOT enforce all of Rule 1.
  Rule 1 has two halves. Checkstyle mechanises the docstring half in full:
  every type, constructor and method at every visibility, overrides
  included, once the scope and allowedAnnotations properties below are set as
  they are. It cannot mechanise the inline-comment half, because judging a
  comment means reading what it means. Specifically, this configuration
  cannot:
    1. judge Rule 1's requirement that comments "must explain WHY a
       decision was made, not WHAT the code does";
    2. detect a comment that merely restates the code it sits beside, which
       is the statement-level WHAT narration the prose standard forbids
       outright in code and configuration files;
    3. verify that "at least one of" the four justification categories named
       by the prose standard has actually been documented, nor that a category
       which IS named is written in the one canonical form, since a singular,
       parenthesised or emphasis-wrapped label passes every module here;
    4. identify which implementation choices are non-obvious, and so it
       cannot detect a non-obvious choice that was left undocumented;
    5. detect a vague rationale anywhere outside a Javadoc summary. The
       forbiddenSummaryFragments pattern on SummaryJavadoc below gives a
       partial grip on that one, and only on summary text.

  Three things carry the half this file cannot carry:
    a. docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md, the prose standard this file
       partially mechanises, and which is AUTHORITATIVE over this file
       wherever the two disagree;
    b. human code review. Rule 1's own Validation Gate puts it there, in
       these words: "Code missing either fails review.";
    c. CONTRIBUTING.md, which points contributors at both.

  Nothing here should be read as claiming that a passing Checkstyle run
  means Rule 1 is satisfied. It means the docstring half is satisfied, and
  the five items enumerated above remain a review obligation.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
  - Trade-offs: severity is error, declared once at Checker level, so a
    missing @param stops the build instead of printing a line nobody
    reads. Rule 1's Validation Gate is written in blocking terms, "Code
    missing either fails review.", and a warning-level documentation gate
    is indistinguishable from no gate at all in a build log that already
    carries other warnings. The cost accepted is real and is worth stating
    plainly: an otherwise-compiling change is blocked by a missing tag.
    The house grants warning-level degradation only where it has declared
    a layer OPTIONAL, as tests/README.md does for the emulated AWS layer;
    documentation here is not optional, so it is not granted that.
  - Alternatives Considered: the binding is on the Maven validate phase,
    not on CI alone. CI-only was rejected because the earliest a
    contributor would then hear about a missing @return is after a push,
    while CONTRIBUTING.md already asks them to "Ensure local tests pass."
    beforehand. Binding to validate puts this gate inside that same local
    step, so the feedback arrives before the push rather than after it.
  - Assumptions: this rule set is written against the module and property
    surface of Checkstyle 13.8.0 specifically. Letting the engine version
    float would not merely risk a new rule appearing; it would silently
    change what this gate means, because module names, property names and
    property defaults do move between releases. JavadocMethod's access
    filter was renamed from scope to accessModifiers at 8.42;
    MissingJavadocType's default token set gained RECORD_DEF; the
    SummaryJavadoc property set changed within the 13.x line. A
    configuration that loads cleanly against one engine can therefore
    fail to load against another, or quietly enforce something different.
  - Trade-offs: pinning costs flexibility. An engine upgrade becomes a
    deliberate, reviewed change instead of something that arrives on its
    own. The house already accepts that same cost for its test toolchain,
    which pins every dependency to an exact version and verifies hashes
    at install time so that runs stay reproducible.
  - Refactoring Rationale: one rule set lives here, outside services/,
    rather than as nine copies inside the nine modules. Nine copies would
    be nine competing sources of truth: a rule tightened in one module and
    forgotten in the other eight drifts apart silently, and no build log
    would ever reveal the divergence. That is the reason the
    config/checkstyle directory exists at all, instead of a file sitting
    under services/. tests/README.md states the same principle for record
    layouts: "never duplicate a layout; keep it single-sourced from
    app/cpy/".
  - Trade-offs: the module set is confined to the Javadoc family plus one
    comment-placement check. Adding naming, whitespace, import-order,
    line-length, metrics or header modules was considered and rejected,
    because the build would then fail on style noise that Rule 1 never
    asks about, and CONTRIBUTING.md asks for the opposite: "Modify the
    source; please focus on the specific change you are contributing. If
    you also reformat all the code, it will be hard for us to focus on
    your change."
  - Assumptions: the house comment idiom used in
    .github/workflows/tests.yml cannot be reproduced literally in XML, and
    the adaptation below is deliberate rather than careless. XML 1.0
    section 2.5 forbids two consecutive hyphens anywhere inside a comment.
    The hyphen mid-rule used in that file is therefore a tilde mid-rule
    here, and the spaced double hyphen it uses as an em-dash is a single
    hyphen, a colon or a semicolon here. Restoring either one does not
    merely look different: it makes this file unparseable, so every build
    that reads it fails before a single Java file has been audited. The
    equals rulers need no adaptation and are kept at the house width.
=============================================================================
-->

<module name="Checker">

  <!--
    Trade-offs: severity is declared once, here, and propagates to every
      child module, so no child restates it and no child can disagree with
      it. The alternative of setting severity per module was rejected: it
      is where a single relaxed entry hides, and a reader would have to
      check all eleven modules to learn whether the gate actually blocks.
      The cost is that this file cannot express a two-tier rule set, which
      is intended. There is deliberately no second, relaxed profile.
    Assumptions: services/pom.xml pairs this with a violation threshold of
      warning and a fail-on-violation flag, so error-severity findings
      fail the build. The two settings agree today; declaring error here is
      what keeps them agreeing.
  -->
  <property name="severity" value="error"/>

  <!--
    Assumptions: only Java carries Javadoc, so fileExtensions narrows the
      audit to the .java extension and keeps Checkstyle from reading the
      .yml, .sql and .md files that also live under services/. Left unset,
      a FileSetCheck such as JavadocPackage inspects every file handed to
      it, and the failures it then reports name files that have no Javadoc
      construct to fix. The other languages in this repository are gated by
      their own tools, each of which discovers its own configuration by
      convention, so none of them needs a rule from this file.
  -->
  <property name="fileExtensions" value="java"/>

  <!--
    Assumptions: sources are UTF-8, which is also the encoding this build
      compiles with. Checkstyle reads each file using the charset named
      here, so a mismatch does not fail loudly: it mis-decodes bytes, and a
      Javadoc summary or a message string containing a non-ASCII character
      is then audited as something other than what javac will compile.
  -->
  <property name="charset" value="UTF-8"/>

  <!--
    Assumptions: the file is addressed through ${config_loc}, which
      Checkstyle expands to the directory holding this rule set, and
      services/pom.xml supplies that value. This matters because the build
      is a nine-module reactor: a bare relative path is resolved against
      whichever directory the build happens to be running in, so it would
      resolve from the services/ aggregator and then fail from each module
      directory one level deeper. Addressing the companion relative to this
      file makes it resolve identically from all ten starting points. The
      house documents an equivalent path-resolution trap at its point of
      use, where tests/README.md records that the coverage rcfile must be
      named explicitly because "the rcfile lives under tests/ (not the repo
      root), so coverage.py will not auto-discover it. Omitting the flag
      silently measures nothing useful."
    Trade-offs: optional is false, so a missing companion file is an error
      that stops the build. Left true, Checkstyle's documented behaviour is
      that the filter "accept all audit events" when the file is absent:
      the build would go green having suppressed nothing and having also
      verified nothing about which suppressions were meant to apply. The
      house names that failure mode directly, in tests/README.md, as "a run
      that verifies NOTHING must not read as green." Failing closed is the
      accepted cost.
    Alternatives Considered: maven-checkstyle-plugin can point at a
      suppressions file itself, through its own suppressions-location
      parameter. That was rejected because it splits one behaviour across
      two files: this rule set would still name a suppressions file while
      the POM named another, and neither file would reveal which one won.
      Keeping the reference here leaves this rule set the sole owner of
      which filters exist and which file each one reads.
  -->
  <module name="SuppressionFilter">
    <property name="file" value="${config_loc}/suppressions.xml"/>
    <property name="optional" value="false"/>
  </module>

  <!--
    Assumptions: this check is a FileSetCheck, not a tree check, so it
      belongs at Checker level. Nesting it inside TreeWalker below is not a
      style preference but a configuration error, and it surfaces as a
      failure to initialise the module rather than as a missing audit.
    Assumptions: it is paired with MissingJavadocPackage inside TreeWalker,
      and the pairing is what satisfies Rule 1's "module entry point"
      clause. This module asserts only that a package-info.java file
      EXISTS; it does not read what is in it. On its own it would pass a
      package-info.java holding nothing but an ordinary block comment,
      which meets the letter of the clause and defeats its intent. The
      companion module asserts that the file carries Javadoc. Removing
      either one leaves the clause half-enforced.
  -->
  <module name="JavadocPackage"/>

  <!--
    Assumptions: TreeWalker is a required container rather than a choice. The
      nine checks inside it all read the parsed syntax tree of a Java file,
      and Checkstyle only builds that tree inside TreeWalker, so none of them
      can be hoisted to Checker level. That constraint is also the whole
      explanation for the placement asymmetry a reader will otherwise stumble
      over: JavadocPackage sits ABOVE this element because it inspects the
      file system for a package-info.java file, while MissingJavadocPackage
      sits INSIDE it because it inspects the Javadoc tree of that file. The
      two are a deliberate pair, split across the boundary for that reason
      and not by oversight.
  -->
  <module name="TreeWalker">

    <!--
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Presence AND completeness, never presence alone.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      Assumptions: the modules below deliberately form two layers. The three
        MissingJavadoc checks ask whether a docstring EXISTS. JavadocType,
        JavadocMethod, NonEmptyAtclauseDescription and SummaryJavadoc then
        ask whether it says anything. Rule 1 lists "Adding docstrings that
        omit parameters, return values, or purpose" among its forbidden
        patterns, so a presence-only rule set would pass, silently and at
        scale, the exact artifact the rule singles out as a violation.
      Trade-offs: the second layer is what makes this gate costly to satisfy,
        and that cost is accepted. A block that exists but documents nothing
        would otherwise register as compliance, which is worse than an
        obviously missing block because it looks finished and so is never
        revisited.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    -->

    <!--
      Assumptions: scope is widened from the default of public all the way to
        private, so EVERY type is governed: public, protected,
        package-private, private and private nested alike. Rule 1's presence
        clause says "every new or modified function, class, and module entry
        point" and names no visibility, so no visibility is exempt here. The
        reasoning runs the other way from the intuition that internal code
        matters less: a mapper or a repository fragment is package-private,
        and a helper type is private, precisely because it is internal, and
        internal is where an undocumented type costs a future reader most.
        Checkstyle's scope property is inclusive downwards, private admits
        everything above it, so this single value covers the whole set.
      Assumptions: the tokens value below is ALREADY this module's default in
        13.8.0, so declaring it changes nothing today. It is written out for
        two reasons that are not about behaviour: it records that the Java 21
        record DTOs carried across the services are governed as types, and it
        pins the set so a later engine with a different default cannot
        quietly narrow what this gate covers. It is not filling a gap in the
        defaults and must not be described as doing so.
      Alternatives Considered: skipAnnotations is deliberately NOT set here,
        and the omission is called out because the upstream documentation's
        own example for this module sets it to SpringBootApplication and
        Configuration. Copying that example would exempt every service's
        Application entry point and every class under a service's config
        package: exactly the module entry points and configuration types that
        Rule 1's presence clause names. The default, which exempts only
        generated code, is kept.
    -->
    <module name="MissingJavadocType">
      <property name="scope" value="private"/>
      <property name="tokens"
                value="INTERFACE_DEF, CLASS_DEF, ENUM_DEF, ANNOTATION_DEF, RECORD_DEF"/>
    </module>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the visibility property on THIS module is named scope. Its
        sibling JavadocMethod below filters with a property named
        accessModifiers instead, and the two are not interchangeable: scope
        does not exist on JavadocMethod, and accessModifiers does not exist
        here. Harmonising the names in either direction does not produce a
        laxer or a stricter gate, it produces a configuration that fails to
        initialise, so the build stops before one file has been audited.
        Both are left exactly as they are for that reason.
      Assumptions: scope is private for the same reason as the type check
        above, so NO method is exempt on grounds of visibility. Private,
        package-private, protected and public methods, and constructors, all
        require a Javadoc block to be PRESENT. This is the presence half of
        the pair; its completeness half, JavadocMethod below, already lists
        private in accessModifiers, and the two have to govern the same set.
        Leaving this one at package while that one reached private was the
        gap this value closes: a private method could then be required to
        have a COMPLETE Javadoc block only if it happened to have one at all,
        which is precisely Rule 1's forbidden shape of a gate that reports
        success while enforcing less than it claims.
      Alternatives Considered: allowMissingPropertyJavadoc is explicitly
        false, and this is the subtle half of Rule 1's accessor allowance.
        That allowance reads "Trivial accessors (getters/setters with no
        logic) may use a single-line docstring", which grants a FORM, not an
        ABSENCE. Setting the property true would permit omitting the
        docstring altogether on a structurally exact accessor, granting more
        than the rule does. A linter honours a permission by not prohibiting
        it, so the single-line form is simply never prohibited here; the
        SingleLineJavadoc entry under EXCLUDED MODULES records the other half
        of this same decision.
      Alternatives Considered: allowedAnnotations is set to the EMPTY list
        rather than left at its default of Override. Leaving the default was
        the earlier arrangement and is rejected: it exempts every method
        annotated Override from needing a Javadoc block at all, and an
        override is a place where documentation earns its keep, because the
        supertype's Javadoc states the contract while only the override can
        state what this implementation does with it: which repository it reads,
        which message text it emits, which baseline paragraph it transcribes. Note the property cannot simply be dropped: an omitted
        allowedAnnotations falls back to Override, so the exemption has to be
        cleared explicitly. Checkstyle still accepts a block whose body is a
        lone inheritDoc tag, so an override that genuinely adds nothing costs
        one line rather than a paragraph.
      Trade-offs: minLineCount is deliberately not set. Setting it would
        exempt short methods by counting lines, inventing a threshold this
        repository has never stated; tests/README.md is explicit that its own
        numeric targets are "grounded recommendations from critical-path
        analysis - not contractual SLAs; the repository defines none". The
        accepted cost is that a one-line method must be documented too.
    -->
    <module name="MissingJavadocMethod">
      <property name="scope" value="private"/>
      <property name="allowMissingPropertyJavadoc" value="false"/>
      <property name="allowedAnnotations" value=""/>
    </module>

    <!--
      Assumptions: this is the second half of the package-info pairing. The
        JavadocPackage check at Checker level asserts only that the file
        exists; this one asserts that the file carries Javadoc. Rule 1
        requires a docstring on a "module entry point", and in Java the
        package-info file IS that entry point, so enforcing existence without
        content would satisfy the clause on paper while leaving the package
        undocumented in fact.
    -->
    <module name="MissingJavadocPackage"/>

    <!--
      Assumptions: scope is private here for the same reason it is on the two
        presence checks above. Completeness and presence have to agree on
        which types they govern, because a type that is required to have a
        Javadoc block but is exempt from having a complete one is the gap
        Rule 1's forbidden pattern describes. Widening one without the other
        is the mistake this repetition is meant to prevent, and it is why all
        four Javadoc modules in this file now name the same widest visibility
        rather than three naming one and the fourth naming another.
      Assumptions: this module exists specifically to close the record DTO
        gap, which is also why it is not redundant with MissingJavadocType
        above. The presence check asks only whether a Javadoc block exists on
        the type. This check reads that block and reports type parameters AND
        record components for which no param tag is present. Because the DTO
        layer across the services is built from Java 21 records, a record
        whose components are undocumented would otherwise ship with a
        one-line block and pass the presence check: precisely Rule 1's
        forbidden pattern of a docstring that omits its parameters.
      Trade-offs: allowMissingParamTags is false, so every record component
        and every type parameter needs a param tag. On a wide DTO, and the
        account update payload is wide, that is a great many tags. The cost
        is accepted because these field-level contracts derive from
        fixed-width copybook layouts, and the param tag is the one place a
        field's provenance and declared width can be stated next to it.
      Assumptions: authorFormat and versionFormat are deliberately left unset,
        so author and version tags are never required. The house convention
        in tests/README.md names exactly four docstring elements, Purpose,
        Parameters, Returns and Exceptions, and neither of those tags is
        among them; requiring a fifth would enforce something that neither
        the house convention nor Rule 1 asks for.
    -->
    <module name="JavadocType">
      <property name="scope" value="private"/>
      <property name="allowMissingParamTags" value="false"/>
    </module>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the visibility property on THIS module is accessModifiers,
        not scope; the note on MissingJavadocMethod above records what
        swapping the two costs. The value written out here is this module's
        default, stated explicitly rather than inherited so that the audited
        set is legible from the file. It names private, and the presence gate
        above now names private as well, so the two agree: a private method
        must have a Javadoc block AND that block must be complete. Read the
        two entries together; neither one alone states the whole
        obligation.
      Assumptions: the three completeness flags map one to one onto Rule 1's
        remaining docstring elements. allowMissingParamTags false enforces
        "Parameters"; allowMissingReturnTag false enforces "Return values";
        validateThrows true enforces "Exceptions or errors".
      Assumptions: validateThrows defaults to false, so it MUST be set here.
        Omitting it drops the Exceptions element silently while the other two
        flags keep working, which is the worst shape of failure available: a
        gate that reports success while enforcing two thirds of what it
        claims.
      Assumptions: validateThrows does NOT police the Exceptions element
        completely, and nothing here should be read as claiming it does.
        Checkstyle holds no model of the exception type hierarchy, so a base
        type and a subtype are treated as unrelated types and both may have
        to be named in the Javadoc. It also does not inspect a throw inside a
        try block that has a catch, where the try block is skipped entirely;
        catch blocks, finally blocks and try blocks without a catch are still
        inspected. Local classes, anonymous classes and lambda bodies are not
        inspected either. Whatever it misses is carried by review, exactly as
        the honest-limits note in the header sets out.
      Assumptions: tokens are left at their default, which already covers
        method definitions, constructors, annotation fields and compact
        record constructors. Nothing is being added and no default is being
        corrected; compact constructors are governed without any declaration
        here.
    -->
    <module name="JavadocMethod">
      <property name="accessModifiers" value="public, protected, package, private"/>
      <property name="allowMissingParamTags" value="false"/>
      <property name="allowMissingReturnTag" value="false"/>
      <property name="validateThrows" value="true"/>
    </module>

    <!--
      Assumptions: this module is what turns Rule 1's word "description" into
        something enforceable. The rule asks for a "description for each
        parameter" and a "description of what is returned", not merely for a
        tag. Without this check, a block carrying a param tag with nothing
        written after it satisfies every tag-presence check above while
        telling a reader nothing at all, and the gate would then be measuring
        tag count rather than documentation.
      Assumptions: javadocTokens are left at their default, which already
        covers param, return, throws and deprecated, so every tag whose
        description Rule 1 cares about is included without declaring the set.
    -->
    <module name="NonEmptyAtclauseDescription"/>

    <!--
      Assumptions: this module carries Rule 1's "Purpose" element. It requires
        a first sentence to exist, which is the one docstring element none of
        the presence or parameter checks above can express. Two of its
        behaviours are worth knowing when reading a failure: a block whose
        summary is only an inheritDoc tag is skipped, and a non-empty return
        tag is accepted in place of a summary sentence.
      Trade-offs: forbiddenSummaryFragments defaults to a pattern matching
        only the empty string, so left alone this module would contribute
        nothing on that axis. The value below is therefore supplied
        deliberately, and every alternative in it is traceable to a source:
          (1) TODO, FIXME and TBD are placeholder markers. A summary that is
              a placeholder is a docstring omitting its purpose, which Rule 1
              names among its forbidden patterns.
          (2) "this is better" and "for performance" are Rule 1's own two
              examples of a vague rationale, taken from the rule verbatim so
              that the gate rejects the exact wording the rule calls out.
          (3) a summary opening "Getter for ..." or "Setter for ..." is the
              canonical no-op summary: it restates the member's own name and
              conveys no purpose, which is Rule 1's first forbidden pattern.
              The optional leading article lets "A getter for ..." match too.
        The accepted cost is a possible false positive: a summary that
        legitimately mentions performance is rejected and has to be
        rephrased. That is tolerable because a summary states what a member
        is for, while rationale belongs in an adjacent comment.
      Assumptions: period is deliberately left at its default. The
        documentation warns that a period not followed by whitespace is
        ignored, so a version string such as v1.0 does not end a sentence,
        but that once the property is set to a non-default value ANY period
        ends the sentence whether whitespace follows it or not. Setting it
        would therefore truncate a summary at the first decimal point it
        happens to contain.
    -->
    <module name="SummaryJavadoc">
      <property name="forbiddenSummaryFragments"
                value="(?:TODO|FIXME|TBD)|this is better|for performance|^\s*(?:A|An|The)?\s*[GgSs]etter"/>
    </module>

    <!--
      Trade-offs: this is the weakest binding in the file, and it is included
        rather than omitted for one reason. Rule 1 enumerates its docstring
        elements in a fixed sequence, purpose then parameters then return
        values then exceptions, and its Validation Gate is meant to be
        checkable against that sequence; a block whose tags arrive in an
        arbitrary order cannot be read against the rule's own checklist at a
        glance. The order this module ships with already follows the rule's
        sequence, so no order is declared.
      Assumptions: this creates no reformatting churn. Every Javadoc block it
        governs is newly authored, so there is no existing Java for it to
        reorder and the usual objection to an ordering rule does not arise.
    -->
    <module name="AtclauseOrder"/>

    <!--
      Assumptions: this is the ONLY part of Rule 1's Inline Comment
        Requirements that a linter can express. The rule asks that comments
        sit "adjacent to the code they explain", and this module is the
        mechanical reading of adjacency: a comment must be indented
        consistently with the statement or block it precedes, so a
        justification cannot drift away from the decision it justifies.
        Everything else in that half of the rule, whether a comment explains
        why rather than what, whether it merely restates the code, and
        whether it names one of the four categories, is not expressible here
        and is carried by the standard and by review, as the honest-limits
        note in the header records.
    -->
    <module name="CommentsIndentation"/>

    <!--
      Refactoring Rationale: two import modules are configured, and they are the
        only two, because a duplicated import is what made a real defect
        invisible. Two supporting authority files each declared
        `import org.springframework.security.authorization.AuthorizationManager;`
        twice - authorization-service's SecurityConfig and reference-service's -
        and the second copy sat several lines below the first, past an unrelated
        import, where no reader comparing the two files would notice it. Nothing
        failed: the compiler accepts a repeated single-type import silently, so
        the duplication survived review of the very files whose subject is who
        may reach which route. These two modules make that duplication, and an
        import left behind by a deleted reference, build failures instead.
      Assumptions: this narrows the header's blanket exclusion of the import
        family rather than reversing it, and the line the narrowing follows is
        whether the finding is a FORMATTING preference or a defect. ImportOrder
        and CustomImportOrder are preferences - they would fail a build over
        where an import sits, which is exactly what CONTRIBUTING.md asks
        contributors not to churn, and the second copy above was in fact
        misordered as well without that being the problem with it. RedundantImport
        and UnusedImports report code that has no effect: a duplicate, an import
        of the current package, an import of java.lang, or a name nothing
        references. Neither can be satisfied by moving a line, and neither can
        fire on code that does something.
      Alternatives Considered: leaving both out and relying on review, which is
        what the header's import-family exclusion previously implied for these
        two as well. Rejected because review is what already missed it, twice, in
        two files; and considered too was adding AvoidStarImport for symmetry,
        rejected because no star import exists anywhere in the reactor, so the
        module would guard a shape nothing has and its first failure would be on
        a style question rather than on a defect.
      Trade-offs: an import that is unused only because the code referencing it
        is not yet authored now fails the build rather than waiting. That is
        accepted, and it is the smaller cost: an unreferenced import is
        indistinguishable from a leftover, so a reader cannot tell a placeholder
        from a mistake, whereas adding the import beside the code that uses it
        costs one line at the moment it is needed.
    -->
    <module name="RedundantImport"/>
    <module name="UnusedImports">
      <!--
        Assumptions: Javadoc references count as usage. A type named only in an
          {@link} or an at-clause is referenced by the documentation this rule set
          exists to enforce, so with this property false every such import would
          be reported and the mechanical repair would be to delete the import and
          break the link. That would set the two halves of Rule 1 against each
          other - a module configured for code hygiene would degrade the very
          Javadoc the rest of this file requires - which is why the property is
          set explicitly here rather than left at its default.
      -->
      <property name="processJavadoc" value="true"/>
    </module>

  </module>

